<h1>Calling exit() in a Suite</h1>
<p>Python's <code>exit()</code> and <code>sys.exit()</code> raise <code>SystemExit</code>, and a plain script ends
	the moment one is raised. Test code sometimes calls it by habit, or through a library that does, and in a suite
	that would be the wrong outcome: one case giving up must not take the rest of the run with it. So the framework
	treats an exit exactly like an uncaught exception. The component the call happened in ends with
	<strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong> and the error description <code>exit() called by user</code>, no
	traceback is logged, and the run goes on with whatever comes next at that level. This page lists every place an
	exit can appear and what it ends there.</p>
<p>The rule behind every row is the one the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/orcraft-statuses/#error">Status:
	Error</a> test shows: an error ends the component it happened in, not the run around it. A case that errors is
	one executed, errored case, counted as an error and not as done, and the next case still runs, because the cause
	may belong to that case alone or be sporadic and gone by the next one.</p>

<h2 id="inside-a-case">Inside a case</h2>
<p>An exit in the case body ends that case:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase) -> OrcraftTestResult:
	if not self.app.is_running():
		exit(3)
	...
	return case.result') }}
<p>The case cycle it happened in is reported as <strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong> with
	<code>exit() called by user</code> as its description, and the case is counted as one executed, errored case.
	Its remaining case cycles still run, the matching <code>_case_teardown</code> still runs, and the next case
	starts as usual. The exit's own code, 3 here, is discarded: when the test is run from the command line, the
	process exits with the framework's error code, the same one an exception produces.</p>

<h2 id="inside-a-step-or-flow">Inside a step or a flow</h2>
<p>An exit inside <code>_run_step</code> ends that step with the same description, whether the step runs locally or
	on an endpoint. On an endpoint the step's result comes back to the runner like any other result, and the
	endpoint application keeps serving. The flow that ran the step receives the errored step result and, if it
	hands it on with <code>handle_errors</code>, ends with the same description; the case that ran the flow does the
	same, and the run continues with the next case.</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_flow(self) -> OrcraftTestFlowResult:
	step_result = self.run_step(StepLaunchApp, app_folder=self.config.app_folder)
	# An exit inside the step arrives here as an ERROR result, not as an exception.
	if self.handle_errors(step_result, self.result):
		return self.result
	...') }}
<p>An exit directly inside <code>_run_flow</code> ends the flow the same way. In both places the outcome is the
	same as raising an exception there, with a cleaner log: no traceback, only the description.</p>

<h2 id="inside-a-lifecycle-hook">Inside a lifecycle hook</h2>
<p>A <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/lifecycle-hooks/">lifecycle hook</a> guards a scope, and an exit in a
	hook ends the scope the hook guards, exactly as raising from it does. The hook's own row reads
	<strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong> with <code>exit() called by user</code>, and the scope's error
	description names the hook, for example <code>Case setup failed: exit() called by user</code>.</p>

<table>
	<thead>
		<tr>
			<th>Exit in</th>
			<th>What ends</th>
			<th>What still runs</th>
		</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td><code>_case_setup</code></td>
			<td>That case: its cycles do not run, its teardown is skipped, and it is counted as an errored case.</td>
			<td>The next case, the cycle teardown, the next test cycle, the test teardown.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>_case_teardown</code></td>
			<td>That case is marked <strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong>, whatever its cycles reported.</td>
			<td>The next case and everything after it.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>_test_cycle_setup</code></td>
			<td>That test cycle: none of its cases run and its cycle teardown is skipped.</td>
			<td>The next test cycle and the test teardown.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>_test_cycle_teardown</code></td>
			<td>That test cycle is marked <strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong>; its cases keep their results.</td>
			<td>The next test cycle and the test teardown.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>_setup</code></td>
			<td>The whole test: no cycle and no case runs, and <code>_teardown</code> is skipped.</td>
			<td>The other tests of the group or suite.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td><code>_teardown</code></td>
			<td>The test is marked <strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong>; its cases keep their results.</td>
			<td>The other tests of the group or suite.</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>

<p>The test's own status is the worst of what happened inside it, so a test with one errored case, cycle or hook
	reads <strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong> even when everything else passed, and its error description
	is the first one recorded. The counters still tell the full story: the cases that ran and passed are counted as
	passed, the errored ones as errors, and none of them as done.</p>

<h2 id="groups-and-suites">Groups and suites</h2>
<p>A group or a suite has no code of its own that runs; it holds tests. Each test runs in its own process, so an
	exit anywhere inside one test, hooks included, ends at most that test. The group or suite records the test as
	<strong class="status-error">ERROR</strong> and runs its other members as planned.</p>

<h2 id="around-the-run">Around the run</h2>
<p>The one place an exit is meant to end the process is the script that runs the suite, after <code>run()</code>
	has returned. That is how a CI job or a script written by an
	<a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/running/">AI agent</a> turns the result into a process exit code:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('if __name__ == "__main__":
	result = MyTest().run()
	sys.exit(0 if result.status is OrcraftStatus.PASS else 1)') }}
<p>Here nothing of the framework is running any more, so the exit does what Python does with it. Inside a component,
	reach for a checkpoint with <code>is_error=True</code> or assign <code>error_description</code> instead of
	calling <code>exit()</code>: the outcome is the same error, with a message that says why.</p>
